This generation tests genuine parsimony from the opposite direction: instead of adding more terms, I test whether the measured D-feed flow (xmeas_2) alone — without the redundant valve-position proxy (xmv_1) — already captures the dominant inflow signal, since xmeas_2 is the more direct physical flux measurement (actual mass flow) while xmv_1 is merely the valve's control signal (subject to installed-characteristic nonlinearity and control noise that Gen-6/Gen-17 already showed doesn't help). Removing the collinear xmv_1 term should reduce coefficient noise/instability from correlated regressors without losing real inflow information, potentially improving the fitness metric if it rewards a simpler, cleaner instance of the F_in − F_out mass-balance law. I retain the previously-validated xmv_10 outflow term and the near-zero constant. I continue to exclude xmeas_21/xmeas_22 (demonstrated CW-loop control-echo confounding per Gen-1's failure), xmeas_40 (composition-analyzer sampling artifact), and the forbidden xmv_3/xmeas_1 (lagging reactive variables). If this simplified two-flux model does not beat Gen-2's fitness, this would confirm that both xmv_1 and xmeas_2 jointly contribute non-redundant information despite their correlation, and Gen-2 should be retained as the final, most robust answer.
